# 2. What is the password of the ZIP file?

## Tool(s):

- [john](https://github.com/openwall/john)

- [Cyberchef]()

## Walkthrough

### Method 1

Extract the authorization information from the zip:

```bash
/home/admin/.src/john/run/zip2john file.zip > file.hash
```

Brute-force with `rockyou.txt` wordlist:

```bash
/home/admin/.src/john/run/john --wordlist="$HOME/Documents/Workspace/CTF/RockYou/rockyou.txt" file.hash
Using default input encoding: UTF-8
Loaded 1 password hash (PKZIP [32/64])
Cracked 1 password hash (is in /home/admin/.src/john/run/john.pot), use "--show"
No password hashes left to crack (see FAQ)
```

Let's see the result with `--show` flag:

```bash
❯ /home/admin/.src/john/run/john --show file.hash
file.zip:redforever::file.zip:README.txt, ssdog1.jpeg:file.zip
         ^^^^^^^^^^
1 password hash cracked, 0 left
```

### Method 2:

Pay attention to the network traffic, you can see there's a Base64 encoded string:

![07](/home/admin/Documents/Workspace/Docs/Markdown/CTF/BTLO/Shiba_Insider/rsrc/07.jpg)

> [The HTTP Authorization request header can be used to provide credentials that authenticate a user agent with a server, allowing access to protected resources.](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Authorization#:~:text=The%20HTTP%20Authorization%20request%20header%20can%20be%20used%20to%20provide%20credentials%20that%20authenticate%20a%20user%20agent%20with%20a%20server%2C%20allowing%20access%20to%20protected%20resources.)

Let's use Cyberchef's Base64 decoding tool:

![08](/home/admin/Documents/Workspace/Docs/Markdown/CTF/BTLO/Shiba_Insider/rsrc/08.jpg)

## Answer: `redforever`

